///1DDT
Help by Willem Aandewiel
DDT - Examine an 8080 program

///2STARTING
  E>A:DDT DEMO.COM

DDT loads the program and displays its own command prompt.
Addresses and values are hexadecimal. Enter the following commands
at the debugger prompt, without a CP/M prompt prefix.

///2EXAMINING
  D100,13F
  L100
  X

D displays memory bytes. L disassembles 8080 instructions.
X displays processor status and registers.

///2EXECUTION
  T
  G100
  G0

T executes one instruction and displays the resulting state.
G100 starts execution at 0100H. It really runs the loaded program.
G0 returns to CP/M through the warm-start entry point.

///2CHANGING
S followed by an address changes memory interactively, for example
S100. Enter a period to finish this input.
Memory changes do not automatically update DEMO.COM on disk.
Use a copy of your program when experimenting.
DDT uses 8080 mnemonics. For Z80 debugging, see ZSID.
